# ListNotification against this project's own test eUICC, captured with
# rsp_record_open -- a real card, not a hand-built payload.
#
# Five pending notifications, and they are a record of this project's own
# history rather than a contrived fixture:
#
#   seq 24-26  notificationAddress "smdp-address-placeholder.invalid"
#              Installs from before euicc-rsp took a real SM-DP+ address.
#              RFC 2606 reserves .invalid so that it can never resolve,
#              which means these three can never be delivered and will
#              sit in the queue until something removes them explicitly.
#              That is what a placeholder costs once a card has seen it.
#   seq 27-28  notificationAddress "127.0.0.1"
#              Downloads from a real SM-DP+ over ES9+.
#
# Every entry is an install. The eUICC generates those regardless; enable,
# disable and delete are only reported when the Profile's own
# notificationConfigurationInfo asks for them, and the metadata
# euicc-tools builds deliberately leaves that field out.
#
# What this fixture pins: the list decodes, seqNumber and the
# NotificationEvent bit come out, the OPTIONAL iccid is present, and the
# address is carried as written.
# A euicc-lpa session, recorded by rsp_record_open: one line per
# direction, hex APDUs (see this project's src/rsp_transport.c for the
# exact format). This is a log, not something to run.
#
# Whether this file is safe to share depends on what was asked of the
# card during the session it captures, and this header cannot know
# that in general -- a read-only query (EID, versions, key
# identifiers) is public; a write session (installing, enabling,
# deleting a profile) can carry protected material. Check what is
# actually below before pasting this into an issue.
